# About civica

I created civica because I wanted Home Assistant to make sense to the whole household. Checking the heating or turning on a lamp should not depend on knowing how the dashboard was built.

civica adapts the GOV.UK Design System for home automation, with its own project identity. A household can use its own name or optional crest or logo. The aim is a clear, predictable interface: readable text, familiar controls and useful information in a consistent place.

civica is an independent project. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by the UK government, the Government Digital Service or Home Assistant.

## Designed for the people sharing a home

I wanted older relatives, neurodivergent people and guests to be part of the design from the start. People have different preferences, experience and access needs. Age or a diagnosis does not tell us which layout will work for someone.

The practical starting point is an everyday task. Someone opening a heating control needs to find the room, understand the current temperature and see what they can change. They should be able to distinguish the reported state from a request that is still waiting for confirmation.

For this project, I chose restrained decoration and a clear order of information. Headings, spacing and plain language help separate the task from supporting detail. The goal is to reduce the effort of finding and understanding a control while keeping important faults and limits visible.

