## Why start with GOV.UK

The [GOV.UK Design System](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/) brings together reusable styles, components and patterns informed by public-service research. Its emphasis on clear tasks, consistent presentation and accessible interaction gives this project a useful starting point.

Those patterns still need adapting and testing in a home. A dashboard is used repeatedly, sometimes for a quick glance and sometimes to investigate a problem. It includes live readings, repeated controls and devices with different capabilities.

civica draws on other public-service systems where they address those needs: NHS guidance for some content and component patterns, MoJ for notification badges, and ONS for chart presentation. The [source register](../community/sources.html) records these influences. Their research informs the design; it does not establish that a particular household dashboard works for everyone.

## Start with the overview, then offer detail

A household overview should make common tasks easy to find. Room names, a few relevant readings and clearly labelled links can provide a useful starting point. A heating status needs a room and a unit. A warning needs an explanation. Colour and icons can support that meaning, but cannot carry it alone.

<figure class="c-project-figure">
