## Adapt the design to Home Assistant

Some home controls need patterns beyond a standard public-service form. A brightness slider, camera image or energy graph can be useful when it serves the task. civica documents these adaptations, including their keyboard behaviour, labels and failure states.

The project has three parts:

- **A theme** supplies shared colours, typography and other presentation settings.
- **An optional interface module and custom cards** add the specific features listed in the [compatibility guide](compatibility.html).
- **A design and writing guide** provides reference patterns and examples for building further household interfaces.

Installing the theme does not implement every example in the guide. Home Assistant continues to manage device capabilities, account permissions, native navigation and translations. Some native and third-party components have styling limits; the compatibility guide identifies the supported adaptations and what still needs checking.

## Accessibility is part of the work

Readable type, visible keyboard focus, sufficient contrast and clear state descriptions are design requirements here. They need to be checked alongside zoom, narrow screens, assistive technology and the tasks people actually carry out.

A theme alone cannot establish that a complete dashboard meets WCAG or works for every person. Installed cards, device behaviour, content and individual needs all matter. Use the [accessibility testing guide](../accessibility/testing.html), record any limitations and involve the people who will use the home controls.

## Credited sources and licensing

The [GOV.UK Design System website](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/) publishes its content under the Open Government Licence v3.0, except where otherwise stated. Its [website code](https://github.com/alphagov/govuk-design-system/blob/main/LICENSE) and [GOV.UK Frontend code](https://github.com/alphagov/govuk-frontend/blob/main/LICENSE.txt) use the MIT licence. These terms have their own attribution and reuse conditions.

Reusable guidance and code do not make civica an official service. This project uses the supplied civica identity and Arial, following the separation described in [GOV.UK's unbranded Frontend guidance](https://frontend.design-system.service.gov.uk/using-govuk-frontend-without-govuk-branding/). See [licensing and attribution](../community/licensing.html) for the project's dependencies, artwork and source notices.

## Try it with a real household task

Start with one task, such as finding a room's heating status or controlling a light. Check it with the people who will use it, including someone unfamiliar with the setup. Use what you learn to choose the information, wording and controls that belong on the page.

- [Install civica](install.html) and check the supported setup.
- [Choose a colour palette](colour-settings.html) and its Vivid or Muted variant.
- [Plan a household overview](../patterns/home.html) with clear routes to detail.
- [Use the writing guide](../content/index.html) for labels, states and explanations.
- [Check compatibility and limits](compatibility.html) before relying on a particular component.
